From 860117f9ee6cde19d8755f91db9edcc79b7493ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ramin Moussavi <lordrasmus@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:21:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] m68k: implement XFmode arithmetic with soft-fp

On m68k without an FPU, long double is the 96-bit Motorola extended
format, and float.h promises LDBL_MANT_DIG 64 and LDBL_MAX_EXP 16384.
The libgcc routines behind it did not deliver that: __addxf3, __subxf3,
__mulxf3 and __divxf3 converted both operands to double and back, and
the comparisons went through __cmpdf2.  So every long double operation
had 53 bits and the exponent range of double, 1 + LDBL_EPSILON compared
equal to 1, values that differ only below the 53rd bit compared equal,
and NaN compared greater than everything.  __truncxfdf2 also truncated
instead of rounding.

The effect is not limited to programs using long double.  A C library
that computes strtod through long double, as uClibc-ng does, lost every
double subnormal: 2^-1074 was scaled by 2^-1024, the conversion to
double made that an infinity, and the result came out as 0.

Replace the extended part of fpgnulib.c with routines built on soft-fp.
soft-fp/extended.h describes the Intel format and cannot be used as is:
the Motorola layout puts the padding between exponent and significand,
and a biased exponent of 0 means J.F * 2^-16383 with the explicit
integer bit honoured, where IEEE treats it as exponent 1.  Denormals
therefore reach 2^-16446, one binade below x87.  The new
config/m68k/xf-format.h handles this by biasing the exponent by 16384
internally and mapping it in FP_UNPACK_RAW_E and FP_PACK_RAW_E; the
extra exponent range this leaves at the top is turned into infinity on
packing, which is the correctly rounded result in round-to-nearest, the
only mode this soft-float library has.

sfp-machine.h uses 64-bit words.  With 32-bit words XFmode needs four,
and _FP_ADD_INTERNAL then leaves the borrow of a cancelling subtraction
in the word above _FP_FRAC_HIGH_E, which only clears the implicit bit's
own word; with two 64-bit words the high word is the top word, as for
DFmode on every 32-bit target.  No other target uses soft-fp for XFmode
arithmetic, so this path was not exercised before.

Tested on m68000 code under qemu-m68k against MPFR set to the Motorola
exponent range, with subnormalization: about 6 million cases of add,
sub, mul, div, all comparisons, neg, conversions from and to SFmode,
DFmode and SImode, including some 22000 subnormal and 40000 overflowing
results, all bit-exact.  The uClibc-ng test suite on an m68000 target
(qemu m68328) goes from 4 failures (basic-test, strtod, strtold,
tst-printf-strtod) to none.  The ColdFire multilibs still get empty
objects, as before.

